<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lee de Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lee de Forest was an American inventor and electrical engineer who in 1908 invented the three-element Audion triode vacuum tube, the first practical electronic amplifier. Born in Council Bluffs, Iowa, he earned a doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> in 1899. The Audion helped start the Electronic Age and made radio broadcasting and long-distance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> lines possible; from 1921 he worked on Phonofilm, an optical sound-on-film process. He held over 300 patents, spent much of his income on patent lawsuits and was tried for mail fraud and acquitted. He received the 1922 IRE Medal of Honor and died in Hollywood in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lee-de-forest.jpg" alt="Lee de Forest" width="245" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Thbusch at English Wikipedia. Later versions were uploaded by Neutrality at en.wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lee_De_Forest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
